---
// Reference / CLI / agents.
//
// Written rather than generated: the binary's own surface. This is also the
// command that produces the data the generated reference is built from, so it
// is worth saying plainly that the catalog here and the catalog on this site
// come from the same place.
//
// Counts are deliberately absent: verify-reference-ia rejects a hand-written
// command or error total, because the whole point is that nothing here is
// transcribed.
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CliBlock from '../../../components/docs/CliBlock.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  editPath="src/pages/docs/reference/agents.astro"
  title="agents"
  description="What the binary can tell a coding agent about itself: an offline guide, the command catalog, the error registry, and repo onboarding files."
  section="Reference"
>
  <h1>agents</h1>
  <p>
    <code>strata agents</code> is how the binary describes itself. Everything it prints is matched to
    the build you are holding and needs no network, which is the point: an agent working against Strata
    can ask the installed binary what exists instead of guessing from training data.
  </p>

  <h2>guide</h2>
  <CliBlock>strata agents guide</CliBlock>
  <p>
    A complete usage guide in markdown, written for an agent rather than a reader: what the database
    is, which primitive to reach for, how branches and as-of reads work, and how errors are shaped.
    It is version-matched, so the guide a 1.2.4 binary prints describes 1.2.4.
  </p>

  <h2>commands and errors</h2>
  <CliBlock>strata agents commands strata agents errors</CliBlock>
  <p>
    The machine-readable command catalog, and the public error-code registry. These are the same two
    sources the reference on this site is generated from, which is why the <a href="/docs/reference"
      >command reference</a
    > and <a href="/docs/reference/errors">error codes</a> cannot disagree with your binary for long:
    both sides come from the release.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a page here and your installed binary ever do disagree, the binary is right. It is the thing
    answering your calls.
  </p>

  <h2>skill</h2>
  <p>
    Prints an agent skill: a short, structured description of when to use Strata and how, in the
    format coding agents load. Printing it is the default; <code>--write</code> installs it.
  </p>
  <CliBlock
    >strata agents skill strata agents skill --write strata agents skill --write --for cursor strata
    agents skill --write --for all</CliBlock
  >
  <p>
    <code>--for</code> chooses the target and is repeatable: <code>claude</code> writes
    <code>.claude/skills/strata/SKILL.md</code>, <code>cursor</code> writes
    <code>.cursor/rules/strata.mdc</code>, and <code>codex</code> writes a marker-delimited section into
    <code>AGENTS.md</code>. It defaults to <code>claude</code>. An existing file whose content
    differs is left alone unless you pass <code>--force</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>init</h2>
  <CliBlock>strata agents init</CliBlock>
  <CliBlock variant="output">wrote 1 file ./.strata/AGENTS.md</CliBlock>
  <p>
    Writes repo onboarding into <code>.strata/AGENTS.md</code>. On its own it only creates that file
    and tells you what it wrote. <code>--apply</code> goes further and appends a pointer block to the
    repository's existing <code>AGENTS.md</code> or <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, so an agent that already
    reads one of those is sent to the Strata notes without you editing anything by hand.
  </p>
  <p>
    Note that <code>strata agents init</code> and <a href="/docs/reference/init">strata init</a> are unrelated:
    one writes files into the repository you are standing in, the other prepares the home directory.
  </p>

  <h2>Which of these to reach for</h2>
  <p>
    For a human reading along, <code>guide</code>. For a program deciding what it may call,
    <code>commands</code> and <code>errors</code>, in <code>--json</code>. For setting a repository
    up once so every agent that opens it starts informed, <code>init --apply</code> and
    <code>skill --write</code>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
